course: dictionary
lesson: 5039
title: Injustice and fairness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Injustice is a noun. It means unfairness: people are not treated equally, or they do not get what they deserve. It can describe a whole situation, or one unfair act. «Being wrongly blamed adds a feeling of injustice that lasts after others forget.» Here the injustice is the unfair blame itself.
explain: Context gives you clues. «Prophets were messengers who spoke God's word, warned against injustice and gave hope.» You warn people about things that are wrong, so injustice must be something bad. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try "unfairness" in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Injustice has a plural, injustices. Some phrases help too. "Fight injustice" means try to stop unfairness. "Do someone an injustice" means judge them unfairly, before you know the whole truth.
fact: Injustice means unfairness, when people are not treated equally or do not get what they deserve.
fact: An injustice is one particular unfair act, such as punishing the wrong person.
fact: «Almost every tradition agrees that punishing the innocent is a serious injustice.» Innocent means not guilty.
fact: Boudica, queen of the Iceni in Britain, led a big revolt after the Romans treated her family and people badly. Britain remembers her as a symbol of courage against injustice.
fact: «A sense of injustice» means a feeling that something unfair has happened. «Fight injustice» means work to stop it.
exercise: What does injustice mean? | *Unfairness, when people are not treated equally | A kind and generous gift | A very old building | A happy surprise
exercise: «Being wrongly blamed adds a feeling of injustice that lasts after others forget.» What does "a feeling of injustice" mean? | A feeling of being too hot | *A feeling that something unfair has happened | A feeling of being very tired | A feeling of being given a prize
exercise: What is the plural of injustice? | injusties | *injustices | injusticed | injustice's
test: Mia says, «He did me an injustice when he blamed me for the broken vase.» What does "did me an injustice" mean? | *He judged me unfairly, before he knew the truth | He gave me a present | He asked me to tidy the room | He thanked me for helping
test: Which of these is an injustice? | Helping a neighbour carry the shopping | Saying sorry after a small mistake | Sharing your sweets with a friend | *Punishing an innocent child for something they did not do
exam: Why does Britain remember Boudica? | Because she built a great bridge | Because she was a famous cook | Because she never fought anyone | *Because she showed courage against injustice
